# Kendo Chart Conventions

## Chart wider than its container — pan, do not scroll

When a chart has more categories than fit the container, keep `kendo-chart` at container width and use `[pannable]` with the value axis locked, plus category-axis `min`/`max` for the visible window.

Do not wrap `kendo-chart` in an `overflow-x` container and do not stretch it with a computed `min-width`. The legend, value-axis labels, and axis titles live in the same SVG as the plot area; widening the SVG scrolls them out of view along with the categories.

```html
<!-- correct — container-width chart, categories windowed -->
<kendo-chart [pannable]="{ lock: 'y' }" [zoomable]="{ mousewheel: { lock: 'y' } }">
  <kendo-chart-category-axis>
    <kendo-chart-category-axis-item [min]="windowStart()" [max]="windowEnd()">
    </kendo-chart-category-axis-item>
  </kendo-chart-category-axis>
</kendo-chart>
```

```html
<!-- wrong — axis titles and legend scroll away with the plot -->
<div class="chart-scroll">
  <kendo-chart [style.min-width.px]="categoryCount() * 80"></kendo-chart>
</div>
```

## A pan window must not rescale the plot

When category-axis `min`/`max` act as a visible pan window, the window is the only thing that changes as the user pans. Two bindings have to be pinned or Kendo re-fits them to whatever is currently in view:

- **Value axis.** Bind `min`/`max` from the full series, not the visible window. Left unbound, Kendo auto-fits the value axis to the visible points, so the same bar changes height as it scrolls.
- **Category labels.** Use a fixed `rotation` (for example `-90`). `rotation: 'auto'` re-measures per window and snaps the label band height, shifting the plot area.

When category-axis `min`/`max` window a non-justified column or bar series:

- Bind `min` to the window start and `max` to start + span, with no half-category inset.
- Read the `axisRanges` min from `dragEnd` / `zoomEnd` directly as the new start.

Kendo gives category `i` the slot `i` to `i + 1`; a negative `min` floors to `-1`, so `categories.slice(-1, max)` returns nothing and every label disappears.

Apply a zoom span only from `zoomEnd`. Do not recompute the window from `dragEnd` or from a companion overflow scroller — panning must not change how many categories are in view.

```typescript
// value-axis range comes from every point, not the visible window
readonly valueAxisMax = computed(() =>
  Math.max(...this.allPoints().map((point) => point.value), 0)
);
```

Take the value axis `majorUnit` and the maximum used when there are no points from the values that chart already uses; do not invent a shared default for either.

## Custom column visuals ignore `colorField`

When a Kendo column or bar series uses a custom Path `visual`, bind series `[color]`. `colorField` colors the default renderer only and does not populate `args.options.color` / `args.series.color`, so the visual and the legend fall through to the theme palette.

Draw that Path with `stroke.width: 0`. The default column renderer also strokes a 1px border even when fill is transparent; `[border]="{ width: 0 }"` does not replace a no-stroke Path. Apply the no-stroke visual to every stacked segment in the range, not only the top series.

## Clone state-owned chart data before binding

Kendo charts may mutate series data during aggregation. When chart data comes from NgRx state or `store.selectSignal()`, return cloned points before binding to `[data]`:

```typescript
readonly chartData = computed(() =>
  (this.insights() ?? []).map((point) => ({ ...point }))
);
```

Do not pass state-owned arrays or point objects directly to the Chart. The frozen-input regression spec and real-Chart harness are in `{CURSOR}/skills/unit-test-guidance/client-kendo.md`.
